WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.387
Checkpoint 3 asks you to connect the ideas.

00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:09.227
Begin with data quality. What could be missing, duplicated, invalid, or implausible?

00:00:09.227 --> 00:00:17.152
Then classify the variables. Next, choose a summary and visualization that match the measurement meaning.

00:00:17.152 --> 00:00:21.202
Finally, recommend a technical representation or storage choice.

00:00:21.202 --> 00:00:23.627
There can be more than one reasonable answer.

00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:31.272
The important requirement is that the choices are consistent and justified.

00:00:31.272 --> 00:00:33.812
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:40.712
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:43.097
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:43.097 --> 00:00:49.892
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:49.892 --> 00:00:57.282
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:57.282 --> 00:01:01.882
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:01.882 --> 00:01:06.435
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
